es at 4Â°C. The resin was washed twice with 1ml of RIPA buffer, once with 1 ml of 1M KCl (incubation for 2 minutes at RT), then once with 0.1 M Na2CO3 (incubation for 10 seconds at RT), then twice in a buffer containing 10mM Tris-HCl pH 8.0 and 2M (incubation for 10 seconds at RT) and, finally, washed twice with 1 ml of RIPA buffer. The supernatant was removed, and the resin was resuspended in 100 Âµl of 1x Sample buffer with 2mM biotin and 20mM DTT. Finally, the samples were heated for 10 minutes at 95C. Immunoprecipitation The cleared lysates were incubated with 20 Î¼L of anti-FLAG M2 Affinity Gel (Sig-ma-Aldrich, USA) or Pierce Protein A/G Agarose (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Waltham, MA, USA) slurry at 4 Â°C for overnight. Following incubation, agarose beads with immu-nocomplexes were washed with TNE buffer five times, and immunocomplexes were eluted from agarose beads with sample buffer (65.8 mM Tris HCl, pH 6.8, 10% glycerol, 1% SDS, 0.01% bromophenol blue) at 65 Â°C for 5 min. The supernatants were treated with 5 Î¼L of 2-mercaptoethanol at 95 Â°C for 5 min. Supernatants containing immunocomplexes were resolved by sodium dodecyl sulfateâ€“polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE) and obtained gels were stained using Coomassie blue. Mass Spectrometry Analysis The strips from the bands stained by Coomassie blue were excised and subjected to a trypsin in-gel digestion procedure. After overnight tryptic digestion, the resulting peptides were extracted from the gel blocks. Samples were loaded to in house-made trap column (20 Ã— 0.1 mm), packed with Inertsil ODS3 3 Î¼M sorbent (GL Sciences, Tokyo, Japan), in the loading mobile phase (2% acetonitrile (ACN), 98% H2O, 0.1% TFA) at flow rate 10 Î¼L/min and separated in a in house-made fused-silica column (300 Ã— 0.1 mm) packed with Reprosil PUR C18AQ 1.9 at RT into an emitter made using P2000 Laser Puller. Re-verse-phase chromatography was performed using an Ultimate 3000 Nano LC System, which was connected to the Orbitrap Q Exactive Plus mass spectrometer via a nanoelectrospray source. As mobile phase A, water containing 0.1% (v/v) formamide was used and, as mobile phase B, acetonitrile containing 0.1% formamide (v/v), 20% water (v/v) was used. Peptides were eluted from the trap column with a linear gradient: 3â€“6% of B for 3 min; 6â€“25% of B for 30 min, 25â€“40% of B for 25 min, 40â€“60% of B for 4 min, 60% of B for 3 min, 60â€“99% of B for 0.1 min, 99% B during 10 min, 99â€“2%B for 0.1 min at 500 nL/min flow rate. After each gradient run, the column was preequilibrated with the buffer A for 10 min. MS data was collected in DDA mode. MS1 parameters were the following: resolutionâ€”70 K, scan rangeâ€”350â€“1500, max injection timeâ€”30 s, AGC targetâ€”3 Ã— 106. Ions were isolated with 1.4 m/z window, preferred peptide match and isotope exclusion. Dynamic exclusion was set to 30 s. MS2 fragmentation was performed in HCD mode at 17.5 K resolution with normalized colli-sion energy (NCE) of 29%, max injection time of 50 s, AGC targetâ€”2 Ã— 105, loop countâ€”10. Other settings were the following: charge exclusionâ€”unassigned, 1, >7.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
